From: limina <gustavo@noronha.eti.br>
Subject: [PATCH] drm/virtio: accept ARGB8888 on the primary plane (limina direct scanout)

The primary plane advertises only XRGB8888, so a compositor can never
direct-scanout client buffers with an alpha channel: mutter's scanout
test ("DRM format AR24 (0x0) not supported by primary plane") fails and
every fullscreen frame takes the full composition path. Wayland clients
routinely pick ARGB8888 EGL configs (Firefox does), so in practice
fullscreen direct scanout never engages on virtio-gpu.

Real display hardware accepts ARGB on the primary plane and simply
ignores the alpha channel when there is nothing to blend below it
(DRM_BLEND_PIXEL_NONE semantics). virtio_gpu_translate_format() already
maps ARGB8888 (the cursor plane uses it), so accepting it on the primary
plane is just a format-list entry; the host display side is expected to
treat the topmost scanout as opaque, which limina's presenter does.

Two-tier note: this rides the limina enhanced-tier kernel; a stock kernel
keeps composited fullscreen (correct, just more guest GPU/CPU work).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/virtio/virtgpu_plane.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/virtio/virtgpu_plane.c
@@ -31,6 +31,7 @@

 static const uint32_t virtio_gpu_formats[] = {
 	DRM_FORMAT_HOST_XRGB8888,
+	DRM_FORMAT_HOST_ARGB8888,
 };

 static const uint32_t virtio_gpu_cursor_formats[] = {
